# The map's probe, as instruction selection sees it - Implementation-Map.md §4.1 and §4.2, and the
# assertion §9 of that document asks for by name.
#
# Three facts about the emitted bytes, and none of them is visible one layer up:
#
#   - the group is a **16-byte** load and a **16-lane** compare, at every feature level. `Vec(U8)` is
#     thirty-two lanes at x86-64-v3 and sixty-four at v4, and native findings §9.2 measured a
#     thirty-two-lane control group against a sixteen-lane one over the whole map corpus: lookup does
#     not move, misses get 8-10% worse and build 7-8%, because a control byte holds seven hash bits
#     and doubling the lanes doubles the false-positive rate. The probe writes the width it wants -
#     `vectorAt(p) :: Vec(U8, 16)` and `splat(b) :: Vec(U8, 16)` - and this is where the pin is
#     checked. It used to need two declarations of its own, `Native.loadGroup` and
#     `Native.splatGroup`, because a count could not be asked for in a result position.
#   - the tag test is `pcmpeqb` and **one** `pmovmskb`. The walk over several matching lanes is then
#     integer arithmetic on the bitmap - `hits & (hits - 1)` - rather than a mask operation per lane.
#   - the group load never overreads. §1's index run ends in a cloned copy of its first sixteen
#     control bytes, so a sixteen-byte load at any slot is inside the allocation and this is
#     `vectorAt` rather than `vectorPast`.
#
# Transcribed from `resolve/Map.yana.lower.expect` rather than invented: the shape here is what
# `indexFind(I32, I32)` lowers to, with the entry compare and the miss arm cut down to what makes the
# group test reachable. The full path is asserted by that fixture; this one is about the bytes.

# The tag match and the walk over its bits. `%control` is the head of the index run and `%pos` the
# slot the hash started at - both already masked by the caller.
probeGroup(%control: Ptr, %pos: I64, %tag: I32): I64 {
    b_0 {
        %group = load %control, 16: i8x16
        %splat = vsplat %tag: i8x16
        %hits = cmp_eq %group, %splat: m8x16
        %bits = vreduce_bits %hits: I32
        jmp b_1
    }

    b_1 {
        %live = phi [b_0, %bits], [b_2, %next]: I32
        %any = cmp_neq %live, 0: I32
        je %any, b_3, b_4
    }

    # The lane, and the slot it names. `cttz` is `bsf` at this project's baseline.
    b_3 {
        %lane = cttz %live: I32
        %wide = sext %lane: I64
        %slot = add %pos, %wide: I64
        ret %slot
    }

    # Clearing the lane just tested: two integer instructions and no second movemask.
    b_2 {
        %less = sub %live, 1: I32
        %next = and %live, %less: I32
        jmp b_1
    }

    b_4 { jmp b_2 }
}

# The empty test that ends a miss, over the same group. Written as a second consumer of one mask so
# that the movemask is placed once - §37 of test/bench/findings.md, which is what makes `any` and the
# tag walk share an instruction rather than each emitting a `pmovmskb`.
probeEmpty(%control: Ptr): I32 {
    b_0 {
        %group = load %control, 16: i8x16
        %empty = vsplat 128: i8x16
        %free = cmp_eq %group, %empty: m8x16
        %bits = vreduce_bits %free: I32
        %none = cmp_eq %bits, 0: I32
        ret %none
    }
}
